{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply an upward level shift (small in week 1, larger in weeks 2–4).",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: the model reacts too slowly to bullish reversals; after a local bottom it underestimates the subsequent rebound. Add a momentum term when the last 5–10 days show rising prices.",
    "Horizon-dependent underestimation: errors grow with forecast horizon (week 3–4 most biased). Use a ramping correction that increases with lead time (e.g., linear increase in uplift by day).",
    "Missed upside volatility: the model dampens rallies and fails to capture late-horizon spikes (e.g., days ~20–30). Inflate positive moves when recent realized volatility is elevated.",
    "Asymmetry: downside is captured reasonably in the first few days, but upside is consistently undercalled thereafter. Use asymmetric correction (stronger uplift when forecast path is flat/down but recent realized path is up)."
  ]
}
